# Go source must stay LF in the working tree on every platform.
#
# gofmt always emits LF. With core.autocrlf=true (the Windows default), a
# checkout rewrites .go files to CRLF, and gofmt then reports every one of them
# as unformatted even when the content is byte-for-byte correct — so
# `make fmtcheck` and `make validate` fail on a clean tree, and `gofmt -l`
# output becomes useless noise.
#
# Pinning eol=lf keeps gofmt, `make fmtcheck`, and any future golangci-lint
# gate behaving identically on Windows, macOS, Linux, and CI.
*.go text eol=lf

# Same reasoning for the Go module files, which the toolchain rewrites.
go.mod text eol=lf
go.sum text eol=lf
